.SS Abstract
This header provides a \f[B]minimal abstraction for register\-based
argument passing\f[R] on \f[B]32\-bit x86 systems\f[R], using the
compiler\-specific \f[CR]regparm\f[R] calling convention where
supported.
.PP
On classic \f[B]32\-bit x86 (i386)\f[R] systems, the default C calling
convention (\f[CR]cdecl\f[R]) passes \f[B]all function arguments on the
stack\f[R].
Some compilers (notably \f[B]GCC\-compatible\f[R]) provide extensions
that allow \f[B]a limited number of arguments to be passed via CPU
registers\f[R], reducing call overhead for small, frequently called
functions.
.PP
The \f[CR]REGPARM(N)\f[R] macro annotates a function (or function
pointer) to request that up to \f[B]N arguments\f[R] be passed in
registers, when supported by the compiler and target architecture.
.PP
When unsupported, \f[CR]REGPARM(N)\f[R] safely degrades to a no\-op,
preserving source compatibility without altering behavior.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Important\f[R]: \f[CR]regparm\f[R] is \f[B]not a standardized
ABI\f[R].
It is a compiler and architecture\-specific optimization and must be
used consistently across declarations, definitions, and function
pointers.
.RE
